= What Consumer Protection Actually Is

Every purchase is an act of trust — the buyer pays first and hopes. Because
that trust can be betrayed by adulterated oil, false weight, hidden charges
or an appliance that catches fire, societies build defences around the buyer.
*Consumer protection* is the sum of those defences: laws, forums, awareness
and organisations that keep sellers honest and give buyers a remedy when they
are not.

- *Meaning:* consumer protection means *protecting consumers from unfair,
  restrictive and deceptive trade practices* — defective goods, poor
  services, false advertising and exploitation.
- *Who counts as a consumer:* any person who *buys goods or hires services*
  for consideration — for themselves or another person with their permission —
  but *not* for resale or commercial use.

== Why consumer protection matters

- *Consumers are unorganised:* buyers act alone against organised sellers —
  protection balances the mismatch of power.
- *Consumers are often ignorant:* many do not know their rights, the marks
  that certify quality, or the forums that grant relief.
- *Exploitation is widespread:* adulteration, spurious goods, duplicate
  products, hoarding, black marketing, false weights, misleading advertising —
  the list writes itself in every market.
- *From the business's own point of view:* a firm that treats consumers well
  wins *long-term interest and repeat trust*; protection is also *ethical*,
  meets *government and legal expectations*, and answers *social
  responsibility*. Satisfied consumers are the cheapest marketing a firm can
  own.

= The Consumer Protection Act, 2019

The Consumer Protection Act, 2019 *replaced* the 1986 Act — widening coverage
to e-commerce, tightening the machinery, and adding a regulator (the Central
Consumer Protection Authority) for unfair practices. Its heart for the exam
is the *three-tier redressal machinery*.

- *District Commission:* hears claims *up to ₹50 lakh* — the entry door for
  most complaints; appeals go to the State Commission.
- *State Commission:* hears claims of *₹50 lakh to ₹2 crore*; appeals go to
  the National Commission.
- *National Commission:* hears claims *above ₹2 crore*; sits at New Delhi;
  appeals go to the Supreme Court.
- *The two money walls:* ₹50 lakh and ₹2 crore — revised in 2021 — decide the
  tier; the value of the claim picks the door.
- *The time window:* a complaint must be filed *within two years* of the
  cause of action.

