= Common Errors and Special Cases

#exception-box(title: "Common Errors & Special Cases")[
  #table(
    columns: (1.35fr, 1.35fr, 1.2fr),
    table.header[*The mistake students make*][*What is right*][*The why / the trap*],
    ["Asking for a bill is a consumer right"], [Taking the bill is a *responsibility*; the *right to be heard* is the forum's duty], [Rights are what the system owes you; responsibilities are what you owe the system.],
    ["The 1986 Act still governs redressal"], [The *Consumer Protection Act, 2019* replaced it — and covers e-commerce], [Every machinery question answers under the 2019 Act now.],
    ["Any claim goes to any commission"], [The *value of the claim* picks the tier — up to ₹50 lakh District; to ₹2 crore State; above that National], [The two walls decide; memorise them in order.],
    ["A person reselling goods is a consumer"], [A consumer buys for *own use* — resale or commercial use disqualifies], [The shopkeeper buying stock is not a consumer under the Act.],
    ["Complaints can be filed any time"], [The window is *2 years* from the cause of action], [Time-barred complaints fail before the merits are even heard.],
    ["Consumer organisations decide the cases"], [Organisations *file and support* complaints; only the *commissions* decide], [Watchdogs advise and advocate — the benches award relief.],
  )
]

- *Spot the pattern:* this unit's traps are *list swaps* — rights vs
  responsibilities, who files vs who decides, the tiers' money walls. Learn
  each list as a set and test yourself by shuffled items.
- *One special case:* "a shopkeeper buys stock for resale and sues for
  defects" — he is *not a consumer* under the Act; the redressal machinery
  does not open for resale purchases.

